\begin_body

\begin_layout Title
Auctions
\end_layout

\begin_layout Author
Nathan Smooha
\end_layout

\begin_layout Date
September 24, 2013
\end_layout

\begin_layout Standard
\begin_inset space \quad{}
\end_inset

Auctions have been an important aspect of experimental research for over
 twenty years due to their considerable practical as well as theoretical
 importance.
 Auctions serve as a popular mechanism to exchange goods, and they impart
 a simple determination of prices in the absence of intermediate markets.
 Many experiments are concerned with the theoretical issues of auctions,
 as well as providing explanations of observed behavior.
 There are two main categories to the literature on auctions: private value
 and common value auctions.
\end_layout

\begin_layout Standard
\begin_inset space \quad{}
\end_inset

One of the primary topics discussed by research papers concerning auctions
 is the 
\begin_inset Quotes eld
\end_inset

winner's curse.
\begin_inset Quotes erd
\end_inset

 The winner's curse is a phenomenon that may occur in common value auctions
 with incomplete information.
 Kagel and Levin (1986) tried to identify the winner's curse in common value
 auctions when bidders were given either private signals or public information.
 They found that auctions with large numbers of bidders produced more aggressive
 bidding than small numbers leading to negative profits, or the winner's
 curse.
 However, given additional experience and proper feedback, bidders eventually
 learn to avoid the winner's curse.
\end_layout

\begin_layout Standard
\begin_inset space \quad{}
\end_inset

Kagel and Leving (1999) decided to examine the difference between asymmetric
 information structure (AIS) and symmetric information structure (SIS) common
 value auctions.
 In the AIS auctions, a single insider knows the value of the item with
 certainty, while the other bidders are given private signals affiliated
 with the true value of the item.
 As expected, the insider earned greater profits than the other bidders,
 but as the number of bidders increase, the insider bids more aggressively.
 Also, the perfectly informed insider did not reduce the frequency or intensity
 of the winner's curse for inexperienced bidders.
\end_layout

\begin_layout Standard
\begin_inset space \quad{}
\end_inset

Finally, Brown, Plott, and Sullivan (2009), explored the relationship between
 auction environments and the development of tacit collusion.
 They examined how collusion evolves and its necessary conditions.
 Tacit collusion formed under the continuous simultaneous ascending price
 auction.
 Once it was firmly developed, the institution was changed to a simultaneous
 descending price auction, while the other conditions were kept constant.
 This change in institution caused collusion to unravel, and prices returned
 to near competitive levels.
\end_layout

